-- Q4815: Alternative Minimum Tax [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Straight-line depreciation over the regular tax recovery period for the asset","The units-of-production method based on actual usage during the year","The 150% declining balance method over the class life of the property","No depreciation deduction is permitted for alternative minimum tax purposes"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 56(a)(1), tangible personal property depreciated using the 200% declining balance method for regular tax must use the 150% declining balance method over the property''s class life for AMT purposes. The difference creates a timing adjustment in computing AMTI.
Wrong (A): Straight-line over the regular recovery period is not the required AMT method; Section 56(a)(1) specifies the 150% declining balance method over the class life.
Wrong (B): The units-of-production method is not specified by the Code as the AMT depreciation method for personal property.
Wrong (D): Depreciation is allowed for AMT purposes; the Code requires a different method, not elimination of the deduction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4815;

-- Q4820: Alternative Minimum Tax [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["MTC is applied first and the full $15,000 credit can be used this year","General business credits apply first and the full $15,000 MTC is then allowed","General business credits apply first; the MTC is limited to $2,000 above the TMT floor","No credits may be used because the TMT nearly equals the regular tax liability"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 53(a), the MTC may offset regular tax only to the extent it exceeds the tentative minimum tax. Per Section 38(c), general business credits are applied first. With $60,000 regular tax and $58,000 TMT, the excess is $2,000, limiting the MTC to $2,000.
Wrong (A): The MTC is not applied first; general business credits have priority in the credit ordering sequence under Section 38(c).
Wrong (B): While general business credits are correctly applied first, the full $15,000 MTC cannot be used because the excess of regular tax over TMT is only $2,000.
Wrong (D): Credits can be used; the $2,000 excess of regular tax over TMT permits both $3,000 in general business credits and $2,000 of MTC.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4820;

-- Q5140: Estate and Gift Tax [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Qualifies because the business exceeds 35% of adjusted gross estate; deferral covers business tax only","Does not qualify because Navarro owned less than 50% of the LLC interest","Qualifies but the deferral applies to the entire estate tax liability of $3,000,000","Does not qualify because Section 6166 is limited to sole proprietorships only"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6166(a), estate tax attributable to a closely held business may be paid in installments over up to 14 years if the business exceeds 35% of the adjusted gross estate. Navarro''s 45% LLC interest ($9,900,000 of $22,000,000) exceeds the 35% threshold, and deferral applies only to tax attributable to the business interest.
Wrong (B): Section 6166 does not require majority ownership; a 20% or more interest in a partnership or LLC with 45 or fewer partners satisfies the closely held business test.
Wrong (C): The deferral applies only to estate tax attributable to the closely held business interest, not the entire estate tax liability.
Wrong (D): Section 6166 applies to interests in partnerships, LLCs, and corporations, not just sole proprietorships.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5140;

-- Q5172: Estate and Gift Tax [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The $3,000,000 in lifetime gifts reduces the gross estate to an $8,500,000 taxable estate","The lifetime gifts are disregarded entirely because no gift tax was actually paid","The adjusted taxable gifts increase the applicable exclusion amount by $3,000,000","Adjusted taxable gifts are added to the taxable estate for tentative tax then gift tax is subtracted"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 2001(b), the tentative estate tax is computed on the sum of the taxable estate ($10,500,000) plus adjusted taxable gifts ($3,000,000). The tax that would have been payable on the gifts is then subtracted, ensuring cumulative taxation at the highest marginal rate.
Wrong (A): Adjusted taxable gifts do not reduce the gross estate; they are added to the taxable estate in the tentative tax computation under Section 2001(b).
Wrong (B): Lifetime taxable gifts are not disregarded even when no gift tax was paid; they are included in the estate tax base to maintain progressive rate integrity.
Wrong (C): Adjusted taxable gifts do not increase the exclusion amount; the applicable exclusion is a separate credit applied against the computed tentative tax.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5172;

-- Q5521: Estate and Gift Tax [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To defer capital gains tax on property inherited by a surviving spouse","To avoid generation-skipping transfer tax on distributions from the trust","To allow an estate tax marital deduction for a non-citizen surviving spouse","To shelter the trust income from state income taxation in the spouse''s state"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 2056A, a qualified domestic trust (QDOT) permits the estate to claim the unlimited marital deduction when the surviving spouse is not a U.S. citizen. Without a QDOT, the marital deduction is denied for transfers to non-citizen spouses.
Wrong (A): A QDOT addresses estate tax deferral through the marital deduction, not capital gains tax; inherited property receives a stepped-up basis regardless.
Wrong (B): The QDOT is unrelated to generation-skipping transfer tax; GST is governed by IRC Chapter 13 and applies based on generational assignment.
Wrong (D): A QDOT does not shelter trust income from state income tax; its sole purpose is preserving the federal estate tax marital deduction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5521;

-- Q8125: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No excise tax applies because the lobbying relates to the educational exempt purpose","A 10% excise tax of $7,500 applies under the self-dealing provisions of Section 4941","A 20% excise tax of $15,000 applies as lobbying is a taxable expenditure under Section 4945","A 30% excise tax of $22,500 applies under the failure-to-distribute rules of Section 4942"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 4945(d)(1), any amount paid by a private foundation to influence legislation is a taxable expenditure, regardless of whether the lobbying relates to the foundation''s exempt purpose. The initial excise tax is 20% ($75,000 x 20% = $15,000).
Wrong (A): Section 4945 makes no exception for purpose-related lobbying; private foundations face a near-absolute prohibition on influencing legislation.
Wrong (B): Section 4941 addresses self-dealing between disqualified persons and the foundation, not lobbying expenditures; the applicable provision is Section 4945.
Wrong (D): Section 4942 governs minimum distribution requirements, not lobbying; the 30% rate and Section 4942 are inapplicable here.$EXPL$
