-- Q8390: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$20 per day up to the lesser of $10,500 or 5% of gross receipts","$100 per day for each late day with no maximum cap on the penalty","A flat $500 penalty regardless of the duration of the filing delay","No penalty applies because tax-exempt organizations are exempt from late filing penalties"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6652(c)(1)(A), organizations with gross receipts under $1,186,000 face a penalty of $20 per day the return is late, up to the lesser of $10,500 or 5% of gross receipts. For $400,000 in gross receipts, the cap is $10,500.
Wrong (B): The $100 per day rate applies only to larger organizations with gross receipts of $1,186,000 or more; smaller organizations pay $20 per day.
Wrong (C): There is no flat $500 penalty for late Form 990 filing; the penalty accrues daily up to a statutory maximum.
Wrong (D): Tax-exempt organizations are subject to late filing penalties under Section 6652(c); exemption from income tax does not exempt from filing requirements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 8390;

-- Q8604: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Not subject to UBIT because a two-week period is not regularly carried on","Subject to UBIT because any advertising sales constitute a trade or business","Not subject to UBIT because the total advertising revenue is under $50,000","Subject to UBIT because the advertising activity is repeated each year annually"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 512 and Treas. Reg. Section 1.513-1(c), an activity is ''regularly carried on'' only if its frequency and continuity are comparable to a commercial enterprise. A two-week annual solicitation does not meet this standard when comparable businesses operate year-round.
Wrong (B): While advertising can constitute a trade or business, the ''regularly carried on'' element of the three-part UBIT test must also be satisfied, which it is not here.
Wrong (C): There is no $50,000 revenue threshold exemption for UBIT; the analysis depends on whether the activity meets the three-part test.
Wrong (D): Annual recurrence alone does not make an activity ''regularly carried on''; the duration and frequency within each period are the relevant factors.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 8604;

-- Q8734: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Public charity because it receives grants from government funding sources","Public charity because its total receipts exceed the $1,000,000 threshold","Private foundation because public support is less than one-third of total support","Private foundation because it receives investment income from endowment assets"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 509(a)(1), a 501(c)(3) is classified as a private foundation unless it meets the one-third public support test. Public and government support totals $250,000, which is 25% of the $1,000,000 total support — below the one-third threshold.
Wrong (A): Receiving government grants does not automatically confer public charity status; the grants must constitute at least one-third of total support.
Wrong (B): There is no total receipts threshold that determines public charity status; classification depends on the proportion of public support to total support.
Wrong (D): Receiving investment income does not itself make an organization a private foundation; the classification depends on whether the public support test is met.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 8734;

-- Q8937: Tax-Exempt Organizations [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The expenditure is permitted because it supports the foundation''s educational exempt purpose","The expenditure is subject to the same substantial-part test applying to public charities","A 20% excise tax of $10,000 applies as private foundations are barred from lobbying","The expenditure triggers automatic revocation of the foundation''s tax-exempt status"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 4945(d)(1), any amount paid by a private foundation to influence legislation is a taxable expenditure, subject to a 20% initial excise tax ($50,000 x 20% = $10,000). Unlike public charities, private foundations face a near-absolute prohibition on lobbying.
Wrong (A): Section 4945 makes no exception for lobbying that relates to the foundation''s exempt purpose; the prohibition applies regardless of mission alignment.
Wrong (B): The substantial-part test and Section 501(h) expenditure test apply only to public charities, not to private foundations, which are governed by Section 4945.
Wrong (D): Lobbying does not trigger automatic revocation; it results in excise taxes under Section 4945, with revocation possible only for repeated or willful violations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 8937;

-- Q9002: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It eliminates the prohibition on political campaign activity for the organization","It permits unlimited lobbying expenditures if they are properly documented annually","It replaces the vague substantial-part test with specific dollar-amount safe harbors","It converts lobbying expenses into deductible charitable contributions for donors"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 501(h), eligible public charities may elect the expenditure test, replacing the subjective ''substantial part'' standard with defined dollar limits based on exempt purpose expenditures. This provides a measurable safe harbor for permissible lobbying.
Wrong (A): The Section 501(h) election has no effect on the absolute prohibition against political campaign activity under Section 501(c)(3), which remains in force.
Wrong (B): The expenditure test imposes specific dollar ceilings on lobbying; it does not permit unlimited lobbying regardless of documentation.
Wrong (D): Lobbying expenditures are not converted to deductible contributions; they remain non-deductible under IRC Section 162(e) for the donors.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 9002;

-- Q9004: Tax-Exempt Organizations [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Private benefit applies to outsiders and insiders; inurement applies only to insiders","Private inurement applies to any above-market payment regardless of the recipient''s relationship","Neither doctrine applies because the company provides legitimate services for exempt purposes","Both doctrines are identical in scope, applying equally to insiders and outsiders alike"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 501(c)(3), the private benefit doctrine prohibits more than incidental benefits to any private party, whether insider or outsider. The private inurement prohibition specifically targets benefits flowing to insiders with substantial influence. Here, the unrelated company is an outsider, so private benefit is the relevant concern.
Wrong (B): Private inurement requires an insider relationship; above-market payments to outsiders are analyzed under the broader private benefit doctrine, not inurement.
Wrong (C): Even legitimate services can violate the private benefit doctrine if compensation is significantly above market rates, creating more than incidental private benefit.
Wrong (D): The doctrines differ in scope: private benefit covers all private parties while inurement is limited to insiders with a personal stake in the organization.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 9004;

