-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Automatically a public charity because it holds a valid IRS determination letter","A private operating foundation because the founder provides most of the funding","Classified as a social welfare organization under Section 501(c)(4) instead","Presumed a private foundation until it demonstrates meeting a public support test"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 509(a), all 501(c)(3) organizations are presumed to be private foundations unless they demonstrate qualification as a public charity. With 85% founder funding, New Horizons cannot meet the one-third public support test and must broaden its donor base.
Wrong (A): A determination letter confirms 501(c)(3) status but does not establish public charity classification; that requires meeting a public support test under Section 509(a).
Wrong (B): A private operating foundation is a specific classification requiring direct charitable activities; heavy founder funding alone does not create this classification.
Wrong (C): A 501(c)(3) organization is not reclassified as a 501(c)(4) social welfare organization based on its funding sources; these are distinct exemption categories.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 9317;

-- Q10758: Partnerships [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Form W-2 reporting wages and withholding","Form 1099-MISC reporting miscellaneous payments","Form 1099-DIV reporting dividend distributions","Schedule K-1 (Form 1065) reporting allocations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 6031, a partnership reports each partner''s distributive share of income, deductions, gains, losses, and credits on Schedule K-1 (Form 1065). Each partner uses the K-1 to report these items on their individual return.
Wrong (A): Form W-2 reports wages paid to employees; partners are not employees and receive distributive shares, not wages.
Wrong (B): Form 1099-MISC reports payments to independent contractors and other miscellaneous income, not partnership allocations.
Wrong (C): Form 1099-DIV reports corporate dividend distributions to shareholders, not partnership income allocations to partners.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10758;

-- Q10780: S Corporations [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Northfield may re-elect immediately since the termination was promptly corrected","Northfield is permanently barred from re-electing S status after this termination","Northfield may re-elect after three years starting with the 2028 tax year","Northfield may re-elect for the 2030 tax year or earlier with IRS consent"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1362(g), a corporation whose S election terminates cannot re-elect for five tax years without IRS consent. Since the termination occurred in 2024, the first eligible year without consent is 2030. The IRS may grant earlier re-election for good cause.
Wrong (A): Section 1362(g) imposes a mandatory five-year waiting period; correcting the issue does not waive this requirement when inadvertent termination relief is denied.
Wrong (B): The bar under Section 1362(g) is for five years, not permanent; the corporation may re-elect after the waiting period or earlier with IRS consent.
Wrong (C): The waiting period is five tax years under Section 1362(g), not three; a three-year period is not prescribed by the statute for re-election purposes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10780;
